From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Victor <victor@local>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 00:13:27 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] tp: 0014 rank0 serve mode for dynamic prompt requests

kimi_k3_dist_generate currently only accepts a fixed, pre-known list of
prompts (--prompt-ids or --prompts-file) and exits once it has processed
them. That is fine for benchmarking but cannot back an HTTP server: an
OpenAI-compatible API needs to accept requests arriving at arbitrary
times without a ~6min reload each time.

Adds a rank0-only --serve HOST:PORT flag. After bootstrap+load completes
(unchanged), rank0 opens a second, local, plain-TCP control port and
loops accepting one request at a time instead of iterating a static
prompt list. Each request is one line of semicolon-separated key=value
fields (prompt_ids=1,2,3;n_predict=128;stop_ids=17,42;stream=1). With
stream=1 the response is one "tok=<id>" line per sampled token followed
by a single terminating line (ok=1;generated_ids=...;prefill_s=..;
decode_s=..;stop_reason=..); without it, only the terminating line is
sent. A dead client mid-stream does not abort decode -- the engine
keeps running to completion so rank state stays synchronized for the
next request, it just stops writing to a socket nobody reads any more.
A shutdown=1 request cleanly calls the existing ctr.finish() and exits.

Reuses the exact same one_step()/KV-reset machinery already used by
--prompts-file multi-prompt mode -- only the source of prompts changes
(a socket loop instead of a static file read once at startup). The
existing --prompts-file/--prompt-ids static path is untouched (still
used for the TP3/TP4 benchmarking this recipe reports numbers from);
serve mode is an additional branch taken only when --serve is given.

Not the rank_protocol::Message wire format: that framing exists to
carry cluster-identity fields for inter-rank NCCL bootstrap, not a
request from a local client process. This is a separate, minimal,
line-based protocol with a single caller (the API wrapper) talking to
a strictly sequential engine (one KV cache, no batching) -- one
connection at a time is all there is to gain from.

No behavior change to the existing static multi-prompt/single-prompt
path when --serve is not passed.
